## React Hooks Internals & Memory Model

### Hooks as a Singly-Linked List
Hooks are stored on `fiber.memoizedState` as a linked list:

```javascript
const hook = {
  memoizedState: value, // State value or [cachedValue, deps] for useMemo
  queue: updateQueue,   // Circular linked list of pending actions
  next: nextHook        // Pointer to next hook in same component
};
```

> **Why Rules of Hooks Exist:** React tracks hooks strictly by invocation order. Calling hooks inside conditionals alters the linked-list indexes, breaking state mapping.

### Stale Closures & The Latest Ref Pattern

```jsx
// ❌ Bug: Stale closure in setInterval (count is permanently trapped at 0)
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    console.log(count); // Always 0!
    setCount(count + 1);
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []); // Empty deps captures initial render closure

// ✅ Solution A: Functional state updater
setCount(prev => prev + 1);

// ✅ Solution B: Latest Ref Pattern (for async callbacks)
const countRef = useRef(count);
countRef.current = count;
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    console.log(countRef.current); // Reads fresh ref value!
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

### useMemo vs useCallback
- `useCallback(fn, deps)` is syntactic sugar for `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.
- **Cost**: Both allocate an array on every render and run an $O(n)$ shallow equality loop.
- Only wrap callbacks when passing them to components wrapped in `React.memo` or when they are dependencies in `useEffect`.

## Concurrent React & Non-Urgent Updates

### Urgent vs Non-Urgent Updates
- **Urgent**: Typing into an input, clicking a button, hovering (User expects immediate feedback < 16ms).
- **Non-Urgent / Transition**: Filtering a 10,000-row table, changing tab views, rendering complex charts.

### `useTransition` Implementation
`useTransition` lets you mark state updates as low priority. React interrupts the transition if the user types another character!

```jsx
import { useState, useTransition } from 'react';

function SearchPage() {
  const [input, setInput] = useState('');
  const [filteredList, setFilteredList] = useState([]);
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  const handleChange = (e) => {
    // 1. Urgent update: Input stays 100% responsive without lag
    setInput(e.target.value);

    // 2. Non-urgent update: Yields main thread to user keystrokes
    startTransition(() => {
      const results = expensiveFilter(e.target.value);
      setFilteredList(results);
    });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={input} onChange={handleChange} />
      {isPending && <Spinner />}
      <VirtualList items={filteredList} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

### `useDeferredValue`
When you don't control the state setter (e.g., receiving a prop from a parent), defer the value itself:

```jsx
function SearchResults({ query }) {
  // Defers re-rendering SearchResults until urgent keystrokes finish
  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
  const isStale = query !== deferredQuery;

  return (
    <div style={{ opacity: isStale ? 0.6 : 1 }}>
      <HeavyList query={deferredQuery} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Angular Signals: The Modern Reactivity Model

### Why Signals Over Zone.js?
Historically, Angular relied on **Zone.js** to monkey-patch all browser asynchronous APIs (`setTimeout`, `Promise`, DOM events, XHR). When any async event completed, Zone.js triggered top-down change detection across the entire component tree (`tick()`), leading to overhead in large apps.

**Signals** introduce **fine-grained reactivity**: Angular knows precisely which DOM nodes depend on which signal, updating only that exact slice of UI without full-tree dirty checking.

```
Zone.js Model (Coarse-grained):
Async Event ──► Zone.js intercept ──► Tree-wide dirty check (Root → Leaf)

Signals Model (Fine-grained):
Signal Mutation ──► Reactive Dependency Graph ──► Targeted DOM Update
```

### The Three Signal Primitives

#### 1. Writable Signals
```typescript
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-counter',
  template: `
    <button (click)="increment()">Count: {{ count() }}</button>
  `
})
export class CounterComponent {
  count = signal(0);

  increment() {
    this.count.update(value => value + 1);
  }
}
```

#### 2. Computed Signals (Memoized / Lazy)
```typescript
// Only recomputes when count() changes and is actively read
const doubleCount = computed(() => this.count() * 2);
```

#### 3. Effects (Side Effects)
```typescript
constructor() {
  effect(() => {
    console.log(`Current count is: ${this.count()}`);
    localStorage.setItem('counter_val', this.count().toString());
  });
}
```

### Signals vs RxJS Observables

| Feature | Angular Signals | RxJS Observables |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Values** | Synchronous, always has current value | Async stream over time (0, 1, or many values) |
| **Subscription** | Automatic dependency tracking (no unsub needed!) | Manual subscription management / `async` pipe |
| **Best Used For** | UI State, component reactivity, forms | Websockets, HTTP, complex async debounce/switchMap |

## Angular Change Detection Deep Dive

### Default vs OnPush Change Detection

#### Default Strategy
Every time Zone.js triggers a tick, Angular checks every binding of every active component from top to bottom. For an app with 5,000 expressions, that's 5,000 checks on every keystroke!

#### OnPush Strategy (`ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`)
Angular skips checking a component and its children unless:
1. An **`@Input()` reference changes** (by object reference `===`, not deep mutation).
2. An event originates from the component itself or one of its children (e.g. `(click)`).
3. An Observable bound via the **`async` pipe** emits a new value.
4. Change detection is manually requested via `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()`.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-user-profile',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  template: `<div>{{ user.name }} ({{ user.role }})</div>`
})
export class UserProfileComponent {
  @Input() user!: User;
}
```

### Immutability is Mandatory with OnPush
```typescript
// ❌ Fails to update OnPush component:
this.user.name = 'Abhinav'; // Same memory reference!

// ✅ Triggers OnPush change detection:
this.user = { ...this.user, name: 'Abhinav' }; // New memory reference!
```

### `markForCheck()` vs `detectChanges()`
- **`markForCheck()`**: Marks all ancestors up to the root as dirty. Checked during the next global change detection pass.
- **`detectChanges()`**: Synchronously runs change detection immediately on this component and its children. Use sparingly to avoid layout thrashing.

## JavaScript Event Loop & Concurrency Model

### The Runtime Architecture
JavaScript engine (V8/SpiderMonkey) has a single thread with one Call Stack and one Memory Heap. Concurrency is orchestrated by the browser host environment via the **Event Loop**.

```
┌─────────────────┐       ┌────────────────────────┐
│   Call Stack    │ ───►  │ Web APIs (DOM, Timers) │
└─────────────────┘       └───────────┬────────────┘
         ▲                            │
         │                            ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Microtask Queue: Promises, queueMicrotask    │ ◄── Emptied completely first!
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Macrotask Queue: setTimeout, setInterval, IO │ ◄── 1 task processed per loop
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Output Prediction Puzzle
```javascript
console.log('A');

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log('B');
  Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('C'));
}, 0);

Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => {
    console.log('D');
    queueMicrotask(() => console.log('E'));
  })
  .then(() => console.log('F'));

console.log('G');

// Output: A, G, D, E, F, B, C
```

### Step-by-Step Breakdown
1. Synchronous execution logs `A` and `G`.
2. Microtask queue runs: `D` executes, queues `E`. Then `E` and `F` finish.
3. Call stack and microtasks are completely empty.
4. Event loop dequeues Macrotask: logs `B`, pushes `C` to microtask queue.
5. Microtask queue drains before next frame: logs `C`.

## JavaScript Closures & Memory Management

### What is a Closure?
A closure is the combination of a function bundled together with references to its surrounding state (**lexical environment**). A closure gives an inner function access to an outer function's scope even after the outer function has returned.

```javascript
function createRateLimiter(maxCalls, timeWindowMs) {
  let callCount = 0;
  let resetTime = Date.now() + timeWindowMs;

  // The returned function retains access to callCount & resetTime:
  return function allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now > resetTime) {
      callCount = 0;
      resetTime = now + timeWindowMs;
    }
    if (callCount < maxCalls) {
      callCount++;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  };
}
```

### How V8 Handles Closures & Memory Leaks
V8 stores function closures on the **Heap**, not the Stack. If an outer variable is referenced by any inner closure, V8 retains that variable in memory.

```javascript
// ❌ Memory Leak via Accidental Closure:
function attachListener() {
  const hugeDataPayload = new Array(1000000).fill('secret');

  // Element event listener retains reference to hugeDataPayload forever!
  document.getElementById('btn').addEventListener('click', () => {
    console.log('Button clicked'); 
    // Even if hugeDataPayload is unused here, some JS engines retain the lexical scope!
  });
}
```

## Advanced TypeScript for Production Applications

### 1. Discriminated Unions (The Most Important Pattern)
```typescript
type AsyncState<T> =
  | { status: 'idle'; data: null; error: null }
  | { status: 'loading'; data: null; error: null }
  | { status: 'success'; data: T; error: null }
  | { status: 'error'; data: null; error: Error };

function renderUI(state: AsyncState<User>) {
  switch (state.status) {
    case 'loading': return <Spinner />;
    case 'error': return <Alert message={state.error.message} />; // Type-safe!
    case 'success': return <Profile user={state.data} />;         // Type-safe!
  }
}
```

### 2. Conditional Types with `infer`
Extract the resolved type from any Promise or async function:
```typescript
type AwaitedReturnType<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => Promise<infer R> ? R : never;

async function fetchUser() {
  return { id: 1, name: 'Abhinav', role: 'Staff SWE' };
}

type UserData = AwaitedReturnType<typeof fetchUser>;
// type UserData = { id: number; name: string; role: string; }
```

### 3. Polymorphic Component Props in React
```tsx
type PolymorphicProps<T extends React.ElementType> = {
  as?: T;
  children: React.ReactNode;
} & React.ComponentPropsWithoutRef<T>;

export function Button<T extends React.ElementType = 'button'>({
  as,
  children,
  ...props
}: PolymorphicProps<T>) {
  const Component = as || 'button';
  return <Component className="btn-base" {...props}>{children}</Component>;
}

// Can be used as a native button or an anchor tag with full prop validation:
<Button as="a" href="https://github.com">Portfolio Link</Button>
```
